== Mathematical Origin ==

=== Mechanism 1: Biological Force Detection ===

From [[Psionics]], the geodesic equation with psionic fifth force:

:<math>\frac{D^2 x^\mu}{d\tau^2} = q F^{\mu}{}_\nu\frac{dx^\nu}{d\tau} + p\,\partial^\mu\psi</math>

For a biological system with psionic charge <math>p</math> (non-zero for coherent neural oscillation), the ψ field gradient produces an additional acceleration <math>p\,\nabla\psi</math>. This accelerates the biological ψ oscillation, which manifests as a detectable change in HRV coherence — specifically, RMSSD increases when the biological ψ oscillator is driven by an external ψ gradient (the detected field).

Detection sensitivity:

:<math>\text{SNR}_{\rm HRV} = \frac{p\,|\nabla\psi|_{\rm RMS}}{\sigma_{\rm RMSSD}}\sqrt{N_{\rm cycles}}</math>

where <math>\sigma_{\rm RMSSD}</math> is the measurement noise of RMSSD (≈ 2–5 ms for standard PPG measurement) and <math>N_{\rm cycles}</math> is the number of HRV cycles averaged.

=== Mechanism 2: Electromagnetic Back-EMF Detection ===

From [[ψ Transducer]] (C3), the coupling is bidirectional — a ψ field perturbation modulates the effective permittivity <math>\varepsilon_{\rm eff}(t)</math> of the BST composite, which changes the capacitance between bifilar traces, inducing a back-EMF:

:<math>V_{\rm bEMF}(t) = -\frac{Q_{\rm bifilar}}{C^2}\frac{dC}{dt} = -\frac{Q_{\rm bifilar}}{C^2}\frac{d\varepsilon_{\rm eff}}{dt}\frac{\partial C}{\partial\varepsilon_{\rm eff}}</math>

where <math>Q_{\rm bifilar}</math> is the charge on the bifilar traces and <math>d\varepsilon_{\rm eff}/dt \propto d\psi/dt = \rho_\psi</math> (the ψ charge density from [[ψ Conservation Laws]] A2). The back-EMF is proportional to the time derivative of ψ — it detects ψ activity, not static ψ.

Detection sensitivity (lock-in at <math>\omega_0</math>, bandwidth <math>B</math>):

:<math>\psi_{\rm min}^{\rm (bEMF)} = \frac{V_{\rm noise}}{|dV_{\rm bEMF}/d\psi|} \propto \frac{\sqrt{k_BT\,B}}{|d\varepsilon_{\rm eff}/d\psi| \cdot Q_{\rm bifilar}}</math>

=== Mechanism 3: Resonator Q-Shift Detection ===

From [[ψ Resonator]] (C1) and [[ψ Quality Factor]] (B5), the cavity Q is modified when a ψ-susceptible material (biological tissue) is introduced:

:<math>\frac{1}{Q_{\rm loaded}} = \frac{1}{Q_{\rm shell}} + \frac{1}{Q_{\rm tissue}} = \frac{1}{Q_{\rm shell}} + \frac{\eta_{\rm fill}\,\tan\delta_\psi^{\rm (tissue)}}{\omega}</math>

Measuring Q_loaded vs Q_shell (empty cavity) gives <math>\tan\delta_\psi^{\rm (tissue)}</math> — the tissue's ψ loss tangent. If this differs between baseline and coherent operator states, it confirms state-dependent ψ susceptibility <math>\chi_\psi^{\rm (bio)}</math>.

Detection sensitivity (frequency shift):

:<math>\delta\omega_0 = -\eta_{\rm fill}\,\omega_0\,\frac{\Delta\chi_\psi^{\rm (bio)}}{2}</math>

Minimum detectable <math>\Delta\chi_\psi</math>:

:<math>(\Delta\chi_\psi)_{\rm min} = \frac{2}{\eta_{\rm fill}\,\omega_0}\frac{\sigma_\omega}{\omega_0}</math>

where <math>\sigma_\omega</math> is the VNA frequency resolution (~1 Hz).

